This study employs high-pressure nuclear forward scattering to investigate the intense magnetism of the aurivillius oxide Bi$_5$FeTi$_3$O$_{15}$. At high pressures (around 14.5 GY), we observe a transition from strong to weak or absent local magnetic fields at the Fe$^{3+}$ sites. We analyze these findings in the framework of strong correlation and spin exchange mechanisms, along with the unique magnetoelectric state facilitated by the specific crystal field and lack of local inversion symmetry. The results offer a distinct contrast to X-ray magnetic scattering findings that indicate strong ferromagnetism at lower pressures (~4 GY), providing essential context for the interpretation of other non-local electronic studies of this system. The technique demonstrates that high-pressure NFS is a valuable tool for assessing the electronic and magnetic states of various elements independently. We hope these findings encourage future high-pressure NFS research as a means of exploring the rich functional behavior of multiferromagnetic aurivillius structures.
